# 12. Migration and Repository Integration

Do not build a disconnected configuration universe.

Inspect the existing `.agents-hub` layout first and place new canonical assets into existing logical locations. Fold semantically equivalent rules together instead of creating duplicate policy/registry/security directories.

Preserve existing bootstrap behavior for Claude, Codex, and future agents.

Identify direct-access bypass paths but do not destructively remove them before replacement behavior is proven.

Use controlled migration:

```text
DISCOVER
   ↓
MAP CURRENT STATE
   ↓
VERIFY CURRENT STANDARDS
   ↓
DESIGN AGAINST EXISTING STATE
   ↓
BUILD GATEWAY
   ↓
TEST GATEWAY
   ↓
CONNECT ONE SAFE CAPABILITY
   ↓
VERIFY
   ↓
CONNECT CODEX
   ↓
VERIFY
   ↓
CONNECT CLAUDE
   ↓
VERIFY
   ↓
MIGRATE ADDITIONAL CAPABILITIES
   ↓
VERIFY
   ↓
RETIRE PROVEN-OBSOLETE DUPLICATES SAFELY
```

Do not delete functioning legacy configuration until replacement behavior is verified.

Version one should remain lightweight. Prefer one Gateway service, structured configuration, central policy/catalog, structured audit logs, strong tests, and minimal adapters over unnecessary containers, microservices, distributed infrastructure, or service meshes.

Implementation may use Node.js/TypeScript or Python based on actual environment fit, maintained MCP SDK support, testing support, Windows compatibility, and maintainability. Docker must not be required unless discovery demonstrates a real need.

